  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0.4,
      "text": "P is fixed at (1, 1). Q sits at x = 1 + h, so the two points are always distinct."
    },
    {
      "at": 4.0,
      "text": "The rise is f(1+h) − f(1) and the run is h, giving a secant slope of 2 + h."
    },
    {
      "at": 9.0,
      "text": "As h shrinks the secant keeps pivoting about P toward the blue tangent."
    },
    {
      "at": 12.0,
      "text": "h never becomes zero, so the slope is always defined; the limit it approaches is 2, the slope at the single point P."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "Static formula sheet: for f(x) = x², P = (1, 1), Q = (1 + h, (1 + h)²). Secant slope = ((1+h)² − 1)/h = (2h + h²)/h = 2 + h. Tabulate h = 1, 0.5, 0.1, 0.01 giving slopes 3, 2.5, 2.1, 2.01, and mark that h is never 0, so the limiting tangent slope is 2."
  },
  "accessibility": {
    "alt": "The parabola f(x) = x² with a fixed point P at (1,1) and a movable point Q further along the curve. A red secant line joins P and Q with a dashed rise-and-run triangle, while a dashed blue tangent sits at P. As Q slides back toward P the red line rotates onto the blue tangent.",
